  <notes>
    HISTORY: the ORIGINAL goal and acceptance criteria (superseded 2026-10-06 by the re-scope above).
    Original goal: `POST /api/communications/send` (and `/send-bulk`, which shares the code) sends only documents the
    caller can read and associates the communication only with records the caller may link to; anything else is
    refused with a clear reason before any mail is sent. The outbound archive stores correct Graph identifiers.
    Original criteria: (1) a send whose every attachment the caller can read and every association the caller may link
    to succeeds as before (User and SharedMailbox modes); (2) one attachment the caller cannot read (or that does not
    exist) → 403 with a stable code, nothing sent, no `sprk_communication` row; denied and not-found are
    indistinguishable; (3) one association the caller may not link to → 403, nothing sent, no row; (4) the access check
    itself failing → the request fails closed (no send); (5) `/send-bulk` enforces the same checks (test); (6) seeded
    negative control: with the new check removed, the 403 tests fail; restored, they pass; both outputs recorded;
    (7) archive: an outbound archive stores the Graph item id (not a `sprk_document` id) in `sprk_graphitemid` —
    regression test red before, green after; (8) every other caller of `CommunicationService` is listed with its
    verdict; (9) gates: build 0/0; Communication + Office test scopes green; ArchTests green (incl.
    `RouteAuthorizationGuardTests`); CVE clean; (10) live (after deploy): the Word Email tab still sends the user's own
    document; an attempt with a document id the user cannot read is refused. Criteria 1-6 and 8 are met by UAC-r2
    task 161 (its `CommunicationRecordAuthorizationContractTests`); 7 and the live check carry into the re-scope.

    2026-10-06 (option A run): DONE. Owner chose OPTION A (notes §9). ArchiveOutboundAttachmentsAsync now saves each
    outbound attachment as its own new SPE file in the communication's container (same container call as the .eml and the
    inbound archive, same {C:N}_ naming, ConflictBehavior.Fail + per-send name de-duplication) from the bytes the send
    already read, and the archived sprk_document points at that file; 146's owner and the profile job kept; best-effort
    unchanged. Tests: OutboundAttachmentArchiveTests (4) red on HEAD and on the shared-pointer draft, green on the fix,
    3 seeded removals bite. ArchTests caught the new SPE write sink (flat-path sanitizer at the call site; container
    provenance entry added as ServerDerivedRecord in SpeWriteSinkContainerProvenanceGuardTests). Gates: build 0/0, unit
    Communication|Office + ArchTests (806/806) green, no CVE, publish +653 B (192 files each side). Observation for the owner: a secure-record document attached to an email filed against a non-secure
    record is copied into that communication's (shared) container (notes §10.5). Live checks open (notes §10.6).
    Full record: notes/097-communications-send-authorization.md §10.

    2026-10-06 (re-scoped run): DONE: Word Email tab switched on in deploy-office-addins.yml (code default stays off);
    161's coverage of the Word tab's request recorded (notes §7.3, verdict unaffected); archive pointer fix implemented
    with a regression test (red on HEAD, green on the fix, both send modes); gates green (build 0/0, 1904 unit +
    contract tests, 806 ArchTests, no CVE, publish +735 B at 192 files each side). STILL BLOCKED (new, notes §7.1): the
    instructed fix makes the archived copy and the source document share ONE SPE file. DELETE /api/documents/{id}
    (DocumentCheckoutService) and DocumentContainerRelocator both delete the file a row points at, so deleting or
    relocating the copy would destroy the original document's file. Owner to choose A (copy the bytes into the
    communication's container), B (no duplicate document; the attachment row already links the source) or C (ship
    as is, not recommended). The CommunicationService change and its test stay uncommitted pending that decision.
    Live checks: open.

    2026-10-04: BLOCKED on escalation trigger 3 before any code edit. `/conflict-check` found that
    unified-access-control-r2 task 161 (#1100), commits 7f9159027 and 0f8cc61ea, merged and pushed to
    origin/integ/uac-r2-batch4 but not on master, already authorizes /send and /send-bulk. It checks Read on
    every attachment, AppendTo on every association, sends one 403 code for unknown and denied ids, fails
    closed, and checks the bulk request once. It reuses existing evaluators through
    CommunicationRecordAuthorizationFilter. UAC task 146 also edits ArchiveOutboundAttachmentsAsync but does
    not fix the archive bug. Archive bug confirmed at CommunicationService.cs:2330/2342. The fix should use
    the SOURCE document's own drive and item ids, not ArchiveContainerId. Recommendation: do not deploy the
    Word Email tab until UAC-r2 is on master; re-scope 097 to the archive fix (after UAC lands) and the live
    check. All acceptance criteria are OPEN. Details:
    notes/097-communications-send-authorization.md.
    OWNER DECISION 2026-10-04 (option 1): "yes we can follow your recommendation - ensure we have this fully documented". The authorization is UAC-r2 task 161's (one implementation). The Word Email tab (096) is held off by a build setting (ADDIN_EMAIL_TAB_ENABLED, default off) until 161 is on master and deployed. 097 is re-scoped to the archive fix (after UAC-r2 task 146 lands on the same lines) plus the live checks. Full record: notes/097-communications-send-authorization.md section 6.
  </notes>

